($bc16087$life-home-chores-04$bc16087$,$bc16088$small_gap_both_content$bc16088$,$bc16089$Case dismissed. The bag and the loose ends both leave.$bc16089$,$bc16090$$bc16090$,$bc16091$You share an overflow radar and, more importantly, a similar definition of finished. The bag leaving the kitchen is not enough if the liner is missing or the outdoor bin never returns. That matching endpoint prevents this tiny chore from producing tiny resentments. Your system works because neither person has to become the household trash dispatcher; preserve that ease by making the curbside dates impossible for either of you to miss.$bc16091$,$bc16092$Name the final step you most naturally remember and keep claiming it. Name a different final step you most naturally remember and keep claiming it.$bc16092$,$bc16093$Case dismissed. The bag and the loose ends both leave.$bc16093$,$bc16094$Approved v4 template$bc16094$,$bc16095$Reframe$bc16095$,$bc16096$$bc16096$,$bc16097$Name the final step you most naturally remember and keep claiming it.$bc16097$,$bc16098$Name a different final step you most naturally remember and keep claiming it.$bc16098$,$bc16099$Both of you save the pickup schedule in a shared calendar and attach a ‘Full Cycle = Out, Lined, Curbed, Returned’ checklist to every recurring event.$bc16099$,$bc16100$Case dismissed. The bag and the loose ends both leave.$bc16100$),
($bc16101$life-home-chores-04$bc16101$,$bc16102$small_gap_split$bc16102$,$bc16103$Case dismissed. Urgency now has a visible line.$bc16103$,$bc16104$[A] = dissatisfied with the current setup; [B] = content with it.$bc16104$,$bc16105$[A] experiences a full bin as an open loop demanding immediate attention; [B] sees the same bin as a task that can wait until a more convenient moment. Because your total contributions are similar, the conflict is probably not ‘who does more.’ It is how long an acknowledged mess is allowed to remain active. Set a shared trigger for when delay ends, and [A] no longer has to enforce urgency person by person.$bc16105$,$bc16106$Define the observable threshold that turns ‘later’ into ‘now,’ without making every half-full bin urgent. Act on that threshold consistently, even when your personal tolerance would allow more waiting.$bc16106$,$bc16107$Case dismissed. Urgency now has a visible line.$bc16107$,$bc16108$Approved v4 template$bc16108$,$bc16109$Root Swap$bc16109$,$bc16110$[A] = dissatisfied with the current setup; [B] = content with it.$bc16110$,$bc16111$Define the observable threshold that turns ‘later’ into ‘now,’ without making every half-full bin urgent.$bc16111$,$bc16112$Act on that threshold consistently, even when your personal tolerance would allow more waiting.$bc16112$,$bc16113$Both of you save one ‘Trash Trigger Rule’,for example full lid, visible leak risk, or pickup eve,and schedule a two-week review of whether reminders dropped.$bc16113$,$bc16114$Case dismissed. Urgency now has a visible line.$bc16114$),
